    "q-124300": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124300",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274717/assets/71620d1e3e6691ef6c91382665fd258891984b1e32aeaec122663d65c1ca5b1b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment between the points $(-3,-1)$ to the point $(1,3),$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=1-(-3)=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
